Here is a descriptive summary of each file, categorized by their structural roles within the project.

Backend
1. service.py
This file forms the business logic layer for the Devices module. It contains asynchronous functions (list_devices, create_device, update_device, etc.) that execute the core logic, interact with the database repository, and manage device lifecycle events (like altering a device's status and logging status history). Additionally, it triggers the audit service to record changes to devices.

2. router.py
This acts as the API presentation layer for the Devices module. It defines the FastAPI REST endpoints (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to interact with devices securely. It injects dependencies for database sessions, user authentication, and RBAC to pass validated incoming requests down to the functions in service.py.

3. service.py
Similar to the devices service, this handles the business logic for the Personnel module. It includes logic mapping for adding, updating, and deleting personnel records, as well as fetching their assignment histories, while automatically creating audit logs for CRUD operations.

4. repository.py
This file directly manages standard database operations (SQLAlchemy) for the Personnel module. It contains raw queries matching exact rows in tables such as Person and AssignmentHistory. Notable features includes soft deletion logic, filtering, name searching, and assigning/unassigning personnel.

5. schemas.py
Defines data validation models for the Personnel module using Pydantic. It strictly dictates the required sizes, types, and constraints of data when parsing incoming requests (e.g., PersonCreate, PersonUpdate) as well as structuring how API responses must be returned to the client (PersonResponse, AssignmentHistoryResponse).

6. service.py
The business logic handler for the Inventory module. Its primary focus is managing fitting materials and templates. Notably, along with standard CRUD operations, it contains specialized methods to create new material sets from templates, or to copy entire material sets from a "couple" to another.

7. router.py
The API routing specification for the Inventory module. Much like the other routers, it maps HTTP requests to the inventory/service.py component and requires authentication for actions like material generation, bulk copying schemas, and retrieving suggestions.

8. dependencies.py
Houses FastAPI reusable dependencies, specifically regarding authentication and authorization. It includes get_current_user to extract details from an incoming JWT token and retrieve the user from the database, and require_role, which allows routes to be protected by ensuring the token's authenticated user has specific internal permissions (e.g., "ADMIN").

9. exceptions.py
The centralized module for custom backend exceptions (e.g., NotFoundException, UnauthorizedException, ConflictException). It additionally holds a FastApi registration block (register_exception_handlers) that gracefully catches these specific errors globally and transforms them into standardized HTTP JSONResponses.

10. security.py
Manages cryptographic and token-based logic for the API. It uses Passlib (bcrypt) for one-way password hashing and validation. It also uses jose to encode and decode signed RS256 algorithm JWT access tokens with embedded expiration times and assigned user roles.

Infrastructure & Database
11. docker-compose.yml
The master configuration file that maps out all docker containers required to run the stack. It declares 5 linked applications: an nginx reverse proxy, the React-based frontend, the FastAPI backend, a PostgreSQL db with PostGIS support, and a redis instance, defining volume mappings, networks, and dependency health checks.

12. 01_extensions.sql
An initialization script that executes inside the Postgres container when the database is spun up for the very first time. It installs mission-critical extensions: PostGIS (geospatial), uuid-ossp (UUID generation), pgvector (AI embeddings), and trigram/GIN indexes for advanced searching.

Frontend
13. App.tsx
The simplest root component of the React frontend; its sole responsibility is wrapping the application's top-level router (BrowserRouter) and rendering <AppRoutes />.

14. providers.tsx
Sets up global state and UI context providers for the frontend. It initializes @tanstack/react-query (allowing efficient caching and server state data-fetching) and the Ant Design ConfigProvider to deploy a global dark/glass UI theme across all component sub-trees.

15. Layout.tsx
The application's structural app shell component handling the general User Dashboard wrapper. It binds together the <Sidebar>, the top header (showing logged in context/logout logic), window resizing logic for a collapsed sidebar, and guards the page layout by forcefully redirecting unauthenticated users cleanly using Zustand state checks.

16. Sidebar.tsx
Renders the navigation sidebar using Ant Design's <Menu>. It lists paths out to all functional areas in the dashboard (Devices, Maps, Pairs, AI Assistant, etc.) styled with a dark glassmorphism aesthetic. It tracks the React router location state to highlight the currently active path.

17. authStore.ts
The centralized standard client-side authentication store built with Zustand. It keeps track of the JWT token, user metadata, and isAuthenticated boolean toggle logic. It acts as the bridge between saving and clearing states against localStorage across page reloads.


